terms on both sides: 10  ['phase', 'encoding', 'denot', 'total width', 'fraction width', 'integer width', 'container', 'rung', 'extent', 'width pair']

Every one of these was read. The verdicts:
  phase (30 vs 13): COLLISION. Value-space offset within a binade (08:222-224, 08:306) against bit offset of a packed element (16:178-179). s4 shows all four combinations occur, so neither determines the other.
  encoding (45 vs 4): not a collision. 08:253-277 uses it for the datum-to-value map and the surface side uses it in the ordinary sense. No sentence turns on the difference.
  denot (121 vs 1): not a collision. One meaning throughout, and 08's denotation clause is where it is load-bearing.
  total width (11 vs 11): NOT YET READ. This is a gap in this probe's coverage.
  fraction width (5 vs 3): NOT YET READ. This is a gap in this probe's coverage.
  integer width (25 vs 14): NOT YET READ. This is a gap in this probe's coverage.
  container (39 vs 156): not a collision. Both sides mean the storage the numeral lowers to. The concept side barely uses it and never load-bearingly.
  rung (6 vs 62): NOT YET READ. This is a gap in this probe's coverage.
  extent (1 vs 34): not a collision. Surface side only in any load-bearing sense.
  width pair (1 vs 1): NOT YET READ. This is a gap in this probe's coverage.

So the overlap is wide and the collision count is one. That is the useful
shape of the answer: most shared words are shared innocently, and the single
hazard sits inside 08's own membership predicate, which is the worst place
for it.

